WEBVTT

1
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Anna H.: Let's review Saturday's incident.

2
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Chidi O.: Go ahead, I have the logs open.

3
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Anna H.: Saturday, 09:10 to 09:52. One clinic chain's integration retried in a loop and exhausted the global API rate limit. 212 clinics saw booking errors for 42 minutes; no data was lost.

4
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Anna H.: Shah from support says 31 clinics wrote in during the outage, and every ticket was answered within the hour.

5
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Chidi O.: That's faster than last time.

6
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Anna H.: So why could one client take everyone down?

7
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.000
Chidi O.: I went through the gateway config.

8
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:12.000
Chidi O.: Root cause: limits are global, not per clinic, so one noisy client affects everyone. And the 429 alert only fired on the hourly rollup.

9
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.000
Anna H.: So we were blind for most of it.

10
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.000
Chidi O.: For about forty minutes, yes.

11
00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.000
Anna H.: One chain shouldn't be able to take down 212 clinics again.

12
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.000
Chidi O.: Then the limit has to follow the clinic, not the whole API.

13
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:39.000
Chidi O.: Rate limits become per clinic, with the global one as a backstop: per-clinic limits, this week, nothing else goes in first.

14
00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.000
Anna H.: Agreed. Take it this week.

15
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:55.000
Chidi O.: I'll start on the gateway today.

16
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.000
Anna H.: What about the webhook work?

17
00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:53.000
Chidi O.: It can wait a week.

18
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:11.000
Anna H.: Agreed: the per-clinic limit ships before any new API feature.

19
00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.000
Chidi O.: Deal.

20
00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:29.000
Anna H.: Let's come back to alerting after the timeline.

21
00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.000
Anna H.: Alerting. Why did we hear about it from support first?

22
00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:55.000
Chidi O.: Because the 429 alert only looks at the hourly rollup.

23
00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:23.000
Chidi O.: On alerting, we need a five-minute alert, not the hourly one: 429s above 1% for five minutes.

24
00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:31.000
Anna H.: Add that to the alerting ticket.

25
00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:43.000
Chidi O.: I'll put the threshold there.

26
00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:07.000
Anna H.: A monitoring gap turned a five-minute problem into a 42-minute outage.

27
00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:15.000
Chidi O.: The threshold was never the problem.

28
00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.000
Anna H.: Right. The window was.

29
00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:04.000
Chidi O.: Have the affected clinics heard from us yet?

30
00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:23.000
Anna H.: Only through support tickets so far.

31
00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:47.000
Anna H.: Shah can help with the wording. I'll write the note to the affected clinics today.

32
00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:56.000
Chidi O.: I can pull the list of affected clinics.

33
00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:10.000
Anna H.: Please do. Support usually sends these, so I'll write it with Shah.

34
00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:44.000
Anna H.: Last thing: the chain that caused it.

35
00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:04.000
Chidi O.: Their retry logic has no backoff at all.

36
00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:30.000
Chidi O.: Should the noisy chain get a dedicated limit, or should we ask them to fix their retries? We're waiting on their engineering contact.

37
00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:36.000
Anna H.: Let's wait for their contact before we decide.

38
00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:47.000
Chidi O.: Fine by me.
